+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new service or weighs infrastructure options for larder. These tenets guide the trade-offs
+before a proposal chooses a tool. The same question about who would run another service recurred for sessions,
+background jobs and pantry search in [issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md).
+
+## Tenets
+
+### Keep the services volunteers operate few
+
+Prefer the web app and Postgres already in use; add a service volunteers must operate only when it does something
+Postgres cannot and at least two volunteers can restore it.
+
+A proposal must establish both conditions. A tool being common, fast or easy to set up does not justify another
+service when Postgres can meet the need, and one volunteer who can restore it is not enough. This principle applies
+to services volunteers operate: an external card gateway or a hosted service nobody on the team has to run is
+compatible with it.
+
+The decisions to [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md) apply this tenet; those records hold
+the alternatives and trade-offs for each choice.
